    <div class="result" id="form-result" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="activity__footer">
    <p><strong>Before you switch versions:</strong> without scrolling back up, what did the second box in the date row want &mdash; the month or the day? Now imagine being told the answer by a screen reader, which reads it as &ldquo;edit, blank&rdquo;.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<details class="debrief">
  <summary>What this exercise is showing</summary>

  <p>Nine things are wrong with the first form, and none of them are obvious to somebody who already knows the answers:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>No labels.</strong> Placeholder text vanishes the moment you type, so the only description of the field disappears exactly when you need to check it. A screen reader announces most of these as &ldquo;edit, blank&rdquo;.</li>
    <li><strong>A silent character limit.</strong> The name field stops at 15 characters, so Margaret Okonkwo-Bell becomes &ldquo;Margaret Okonkw&rdquo; and nothing tells you.</li>
    <li><strong>Three unlabelled date boxes</strong> with no hint about order or format.</li>
    <li><strong>Hidden format rules.</strong> The postcode is rejected if it contains a space. Nothing says so, before or after.</li>
    <li><strong>Required marked with an asterisk only</strong>, in pale grey, with no key explaining it.</li>
    <li><strong>No autocomplete</strong>, so nothing can be filled in from the browser or a password manager.</li>
    <li><strong>An error message that names no field</strong> and is not announced, because it is not a live region and focus never moves to it.</li>
    <li><strong>Errors shown with a red border alone</strong>, which anyone who cannot see colour will miss entirely.</li>
    <li><strong>Clear sits next to Submit</strong>, the same size and colour, first in the tab order.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Questions for the group</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>How many attempts did the room take on each version? Say the numbers out loud.</li>
    <li>What did it feel like to be told &ldquo;there was a problem&rdquo; and nothing else, with Margaret still on the line?</li>
    <li>Now picture this form as a benefits claim, a hospital appointment, or the last step before a payment fails.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What good looks like</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Every control has a visible label that stays visible. Placeholders are decoration, never instruction.</li>
    <li>Formats and rules are stated <em>before</em> the field, not discovered through failure.</li>
    <li>Errors appear in a summary at the top that takes focus, each one linking to its field, and again next to the field itself in words.</li>
    <li>The form takes what people type and normalises it. Spaces in a postcode are the form's problem, not the user's.</li>
    <li>Autocomplete attributes are filled in, which is <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/identify-input-purpose.html">1.3.5 Identify Input Purpose</a> and a genuine kindness to anyone typing with one finger.</li>
  </ul>
</details>
